every place in the world sounds unique  these soundscapes  tell us a story about that place they  tell us the ways in which global warming  is beginning to change the natural  soundscape just before the Sun rises at  first light is the dawn chorus the birds  begin to sing and the insects and the  amphibians that's the time of day to go  out and listen I'm a soundscape  ecologist they record animals for a  living this is Sugarloaf State Park  every spring I can probably be found  there once a week recording in the same  spot using the same kind of equipment so  that I can repeat these recordings over  and over again and compare them and I  have quite a archive of material which  shows over time this habitat has been  changing pretty radically there's a  result of global warming and the  California drought  the first recording made in 2004 shows a  very robust habit added with a signature  of a stream in 2014 things were changing  radically it was already the second year  of a major drought in California and in  2015 we had what I call a Silent Spring  over 50% of my collection comes from  habitats that are so radically altered  they are either altogether silent or no  longer be heard in their original form  here's an example from Costa Rica  that's before logging this is after  same spot this is a coral reef the new  allez-vous and Fiji part of it is dying  and part of it is still living here's  what the living part sounds like here's  what the dying part sounds like  hardly any fish sounds  we really don't know what this means in  the long run in the short run were  beginning to lose these soundscapes  because the habitats are just changing  so radically and we're partly to blame  for them when I first began to record  almost fifty years ago I would go out  into the field and just put on a pair of  earphones and sit and listen because it  made me feel good it made me aware of  the living world around me and so I want  to do anything that does that for me  you
 

